{"name":"Ahaetulla Prasina","hex":"#00fa92","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Ahaetulla prasina","blocks":[{"text":"Ahaetulla prasina is an arboreal, moderately venomous species of opisthoglyphous vine snake in the family Colubridae, found in Southern and Southeast Asia. Its common names include the Asian vine snake, Boie's whip snake, Gunther's whip snake, and the Oriental whip snake."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Ahaetulla-prasina-kaeng-krachan-national-park.jpg/330px-Ahaetulla-prasina-kaeng-krachan-national-park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahaetulla_prasina","wikidata":"Q1551982"},{"title":"Ahaetulla","blocks":[{"text":"Ahaetulla, commonly referred to as Asian vine snakes or Asian whip snakes, is a genus of colubrid snakes distributed throughout tropical Asia. They are considered by some scientists to be mildly venomous and are what is commonly termed as 'rear-fanged' or more appropriately, opisthoglyphous, meaning their enlarged teeth or fangs, intended to aid in venom delivery, are located in the back of the upper jaw, instead of in the front as they are in vipers or cobras. As colubrids, Ahaetulla do not possess a true venom gland or a sophisticated venom delivery system. The Duvernoy's gland of this genus, homologous to the venom gland of true venomous snakes, produces a secretion which, though not well studied, is considered not to be medically significant to humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Ahaya.dispar.jpg/330px-Ahaya.dispar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahaetulla","wikidata":"Q602558"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Ahaetulla-prasina-kaeng-krachan-national-park.jpg/500px-Ahaetulla-prasina-kaeng-krachan-national-park.jpg?utm_source=